 ### Merging, Replaying & Rebasing
 
-- **`merge <branch>`**: Fast-forwards when possible, otherwise runs the same line-level three-way merge as `merge-tree` (non-overlapping edits to one file merge cleanly; mode changes merge separately from content; criss-cross histories use a virtual base) and commits the result with the configured identity. Refuses to start on an unmerged index, while a merge is in progress, or when local changes would be overwritten. On conflicts it prints git's messages, writes conflict markers into the working tree, stages everything that merged cleanly, records each conflicted path's stages 1 (base), 2 (ours) and 3 (theirs) in the index exactly as git does, writes `MERGE_HEAD`/`MERGE_MSG` (and `ORIG_HEAD`), and exits 1; `rgit commit` concludes the merge once every conflicted path is resolved with `add`/`rm`/`restore`.
+- **`merge`** (`<commit>`, `--ff|--no-ff|--ff-only`, `--squash`, `--[no-]commit`, `-m <msg>`..., `-F <file>`, `-s ort|recursive|ours`, `-X ours|theirs`, `-n|--no-stat`, `-q`, `--allow-unrelated-histories`, `--abort`, `--continue`, `--quit`): Fast-forwards when possible (`--no-ff` always records a merge commit, `--ff-only` refuses anything else with git's hints), otherwise runs the same line-level three-way merge as `merge-tree` (non-overlapping edits merge cleanly; mode changes merge separately from content; criss-cross histories use a virtual base). `-X ours`/`-X theirs` resolve content conflicts toward one side; `-s ours` records a merge that keeps `HEAD`'s tree. A clean merge is committed (message from `-m`/`-F`, else git's `Merge branch 'x'[ into <branch>]` per `merge.suppressDest`) and followed by git's diffstat and create/delete-mode summary; works on a detached `HEAD` too. `--no-commit` stops before committing; `--squash` stages the merged result, writes `SQUASH_MSG` (git's "Squashed commit of the following:" log) and records no merge, so the next `rgit commit` makes an ordinary commit. Refuses to start on an unmerged index or while a merge or cherry-pick is in progress. On conflicts it prints git's messages, writes conflict markers into the working tree, stages everything that merged cleanly, records each conflicted path's stages 1 (base), 2 (ours) and 3 (theirs) in the index exactly as git does, writes `MERGE_HEAD`/`MERGE_MSG`/`MERGE_MODE`/`AUTO_MERGE` (and `ORIG_HEAD`), and exits 1; `rgit commit` or `merge --continue` concludes the merge once every conflicted path is resolved, `--abort` restores `HEAD`'s version of every path the merge touched (git's `reset --merge`), and `--quit` forgets the merge but keeps the index and working tree. Not supported: octopus merges and the `resolve`/`octopus`/`subtree` strategies; `-X` options other than `ours`/`theirs`; `-s recursive` runs the ort merge (as git 2.50+ does).
 - **`merge-base`** (`<commit> <commit>...`, `-a|--all`, `--is-ancestor`): Finds the best common ancestor(s) of commits, or checks ancestry via exit code.
 - **`rebase`** (`<upstream>`, `-i`, `--continue`, `--abort`, `--skip`, `--edit-todo`): Full interactive rebase with `pick`, `reword`, `edit`, `squash`, `fixup`, and `drop` instructions. Each commit is replayed with `merge`'s line-level merge (labels `HEAD` and `<abbrev> (<subject>)`); a conflict stops with git's messages and index stages, and `--continue` refuses while paths are unmerged.
 - **`cherry-pick <commit>`** (`--no-commit`, `--continue`, `--abort`): Replays a single commit onto current HEAD with state recording, using `merge`'s line-level merge (labels `HEAD` and `<abbrev> (<subject>)`); conflicts leave markers, index stages and git's error and hints, and `--continue` refuses while paths are unmerged.
